WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:04.129
In this section, we're going to take a look at light properties

2
00:00:04.129 --> 00:00:06.330
and settings and how they work.

3
00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.199
So let's get started.

4
00:00:08.199 --> 00:00:10.640
In Unreal, we have several different options

5
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.119
to be able to change the look and feel for your light.

6
00:00:14.119 --> 00:00:16.000
First up, we have intensity, which

7
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.019
controls the overall strength of your light.

8
00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:21.859
We also have intensity in units.

9
00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:24.820
You'll notice in the point light, rec light,

10
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:29.980
and spotlights, we have unitless, candelas, lumens,

11
00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.679
and EVs to choose from.

12
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.659
EVs stand for exposure value, and they're

13
00:00:35.659 --> 00:00:40.289
the relatively newer option in this category.

14
00:00:40.289 --> 00:00:45.479
100 watts equals 1,600 lumens.

15
00:00:45.479 --> 00:00:49.399
EV exposure value, which means you can set your intensities

16
00:00:49.399 --> 00:00:54.450
in a relative stops or power of two increments.

17
00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:59.090
Directional light or sunlight is what it's mainly used for,

18
00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:02.409
can be defined and used in that particular intensity

19
00:01:02.409 --> 00:01:05.579
units as lux.

20
00:01:05.599 --> 00:01:08.159
We have other settings, such as directional lighting

21
00:01:08.159 --> 00:01:12.760
intensity, attenuation, use temperature, source radius,

22
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:18.760
source angle, inverse square fall-off, and IES profiles.

23
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.000
And we're going to talk a little bit about this upfront

24
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.799
so you can understand a little bit how

25
00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:26.159
some of the options that you have at your fingertips.

26
00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:28.200
So let's go ahead and go to Unreal,

27
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.719
and then we'll get back to these slides.

28
00:01:30.719 --> 00:01:33.680
So in Unreal, we have several different lights to choose from.

29
00:01:33.739 --> 00:01:36.900
I just recently just popped a rec light in here.

30
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.459
We're going to move him over to the side.

31
00:01:38.459 --> 00:01:40.709
We'll talk about him in just a bit.

32
00:01:40.709 --> 00:01:43.030
But you'll notice in every single one of your lights,

33
00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:44.790
and this one being a spotlight,

34
00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:46.900
that one being the rec light,

35
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:50.099
you'll notice there is an area of attenuation

36
00:01:50.099 --> 00:01:55.290
that is shown in our light settings here in our details.

37
00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.450
So if I change my attenuation,

38
00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:00.370
it's basically the area of influence.

39
00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:03.450
And you'll see our cone change in the particular case

40
00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:05.569
of the spotlight.

41
00:02:05.569 --> 00:02:06.909
When it comes to the rec light,

42
00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:08.449
you'll also see that change

43
00:02:08.449 --> 00:02:11.870
as I go and decrease or increase this.

44
00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:15.360
This is basically your area of influence.

45
00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.419
We'll see intensity in the case of this rec light.

46
00:02:18.419 --> 00:02:21.099
And we'll see that there's candelas, unitless, lumens,

47
00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:23.479
and EV, as we mentioned before.

48
00:02:23.479 --> 00:02:25.639
You can also control the color.

49
00:02:25.639 --> 00:02:29.020
The source width and the source height are unique

50
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.120
when it comes to the rec light

51
00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.580
and allows me to change its shape.

52
00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.219
Now you'll notice when I change its shape,

53
00:02:35.219 --> 00:02:38.039
it also will lose energy based on size.

54
00:02:38.039 --> 00:02:40.719
This is determined on how strong

55
00:02:40.719 --> 00:02:43.599
you've placed your intensity on that light.

56
00:02:43.599 --> 00:02:44.919
So if I change the shape,

57
00:02:44.919 --> 00:02:48.000
I'm changing the shape of this light over,

58
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.840
you can think of it as over that surface.

59
00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:53.280
And I'm stretching the light beyond the amount of strength

60
00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.500
that's available for the size that it's now become.

61
00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:00.379
The rec light is probably one of the most expensive lights

62
00:03:00.379 --> 00:03:01.699
you can work with.

63
00:03:01.699 --> 00:03:04.500
Because in theory and how it works,

64
00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.520
or I should say more on the way that we have created it,

65
00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.719
is it works like a series of point lights

66
00:03:10.719 --> 00:03:13.379
trapped within this square or rectangle shape,

67
00:03:13.379 --> 00:03:16.020
whatever you choose to make it.

68
00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:17.819
So it's really how that works.

69
00:03:17.819 --> 00:03:20.360
It is the more expensive light.

70
00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:22.300
But I like to use it as much as possible

71
00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:25.830
because it works great as a fill light

72
00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:27.650
and allow you to get some really great lighting

73
00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:30.150
quickly and fast.

74
00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.949
The directional light is typically your primary light

75
00:03:32.969 --> 00:03:34.969
when it comes to your environment lighting.

76
00:03:34.969 --> 00:03:36.689
And I mean that as when it comes to

77
00:03:36.689 --> 00:03:40.789
if you want a more realistic environment output.

78
00:03:40.789 --> 00:03:43.830
Now we are using Lumen, you'll see a little bit of noise

79
00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:46.810
and we'll cover a little bit about that later.

80
00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:49.830
That does happen currently in some aspects in some areas,

81
00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:52.580
especially when it comes to certain materials.

82
00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:55.810
And there are CVARs that can correct some of that.

83
00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:58.270
So I can move my light, my directional light,

84
00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.650
and it works with my atmosphere.

85
00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:03.849
The reason why this is working so nicely and smoothly

86
00:04:04.389 --> 00:04:06.409
is when you first start a scene,

87
00:04:06.409 --> 00:04:10.479
I'm going to create a generic scene really quick.

88
00:04:10.479 --> 00:04:15.500
You can connect your light and your other values together.

89
00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:19.740
So if I just did a quick new level and kept it empty,

90
00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.100
and we'll review this later

91
00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.180
because we want to talk about light mixer

92
00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:25.060
and the environment light mixer.

93
00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:26.220
I'm just going to hit create

94
00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:29.310
and say don't save for the moment.

95
00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:32.139
So I have to go to my window.

96
00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:35.769
I'm going to go to my environment light mixer.

97
00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:38.470
And you'll notice we don't have those things set up

98
00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:43.500
in our scene as our fog and other things in our outliner.

99
00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.389
None of that is showing up,

100
00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:48.829
but you can create things really quickly using this.

101
00:04:48.829 --> 00:04:50.389
And you'll notice there's some dependencies

102
00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:52.759
and we'll talk about this later too.

103
00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:55.680
Create an atmospheric light, create a sky atmosphere,

104
00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.959
create a volumetric light and create fog.

105
00:04:57.959 --> 00:04:59.680
And you can be up and running pretty quickly

106
00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:01.399
using this tool.

107
00:05:01.399 --> 00:05:05.319
Everything gets connected automatically when you're using that

108
00:05:05.319 --> 00:05:06.959
and makes it a lot easier.

109
00:05:06.959 --> 00:05:09.180
And by default too, if you made it from scratch,

110
00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:12.180
it would also have the same type of effect.

111
00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:15.370
But that tool gets you up and running a little fast.

112
00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:18.430
We'll talk about that tool later on too.

113
00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:21.720
Let's go back to our Lumen example.

114
00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:26.060
So if I go back into my Lumen example here,

115
00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:27.540
we talked about how that directional light

116
00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:30.939
can be connected to an atmospheric environment.

117
00:05:30.939 --> 00:05:32.420
Now, one thing you do want to point out,

118
00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:35.480
this is my default clouds that are set in here.

119
00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:37.779
If you wanted to use volumetrics,

120
00:05:37.779 --> 00:05:39.079
make sure that you turn them on

121
00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:41.339
so you have more cloud options.

122
00:05:41.339 --> 00:05:46.920
And you can find that here in your plugins.

123
00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:49.250
So going back to our scene.

124
00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:54.850
Now, we also have another light called the point light.

125
00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:56.509
Now, if I bring my point light in,

126
00:05:56.509 --> 00:05:58.069
I'm going to talk a little bit of the difference

127
00:05:58.069 --> 00:06:01.449
between these lights and softening your shadows.

128
00:06:01.449 --> 00:06:03.980
My point light's ready to go,

129
00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:06.540
but say I want to maybe soften the shadows

130
00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:08.379
that they're creating.

131
00:06:08.379 --> 00:06:12.649
And you'll notice my shadows are a little bit jaggedy here

132
00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:14.680
on the spotlight.

133
00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:16.480
What I can do with this light,

134
00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:21.339
as well as if I wanted to mess with my spotlight,

135
00:06:21.339 --> 00:06:26.500
I can control a thing called our source radius.

136
00:06:27.779 --> 00:06:31.660
As I increase that, you'll notice things get a bit softer

137
00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:34.100
and we also get an actual physical yellow object

138
00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:35.699
that pops up.

139
00:06:35.699 --> 00:06:39.540
The same thing can occur when it comes to our point light.

140
00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:43.470
I can control my source radius

141
00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:46.300
and that can soften up my shadows.

142
00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.660
And it works really well when it comes to Lumen.

143
00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:49.560
Now you gotta be careful,

144
00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.959
make sure you determine how strong you want that to be.

145
00:06:52.959 --> 00:06:55.100
You can end up getting kind of a double shadow

146
00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:57.939
or I should say a very blurred edge shadow.

147
00:06:57.939 --> 00:07:00.259
That may be what you want and it may not.

148
00:07:00.259 --> 00:07:02.540
So you just make sure you punch in the values

149
00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:03.819
that you need.

150
00:07:03.819 --> 00:07:07.060
You'll also notice when I bring the spotlight in here,

151
00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:10.259
he has a few more other options here

152
00:07:10.259 --> 00:07:13.459
and you will get that in certain light scenarios

153
00:07:13.959 --> 00:07:18.040
where you have a thing called a soft source radius.

154
00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:21.199
What that does, and we'll pick on the spotlight here,

155
00:07:21.199 --> 00:07:24.600
that makes it so that you can go in here

156
00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.000
and soften up that reflection.

157
00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:31.800
So it isn't such a harsh object in your reflection.

158
00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:34.000
So you won't have that hotspot type of circle in there.

159
00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:37.069
I can soften that up a little bit.

160
00:07:37.069 --> 00:07:39.449
There's also a thing called source length.

161
00:07:39.449 --> 00:07:42.160
So if I went here with my spotlight,

162
00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:45.339
I can also go in here and control my source length.

163
00:07:45.339 --> 00:07:49.019
And you'll notice we've now changed the shape of that light.

164
00:07:49.019 --> 00:07:56.959
So if I had office lighting, I can rotate this,

165
00:07:56.959 --> 00:08:01.839
move this up, change my soft source radius

166
00:08:01.839 --> 00:08:04.709
and my source radius a little bit.

167
00:08:04.709 --> 00:08:08.399
Maybe make it a little bit smaller.

168
00:08:08.399 --> 00:08:10.810
There you go.

169
00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:12.209
And my soft source radius,

170
00:08:12.209 --> 00:08:13.610
I don't have to worry about this too much

171
00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:16.329
because again, it is the reflectivity primarily,

172
00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:19.250
but the source length will be my primary.

173
00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:23.189
Look here, you can almost make like a poor man's lightsaber

174
00:08:23.189 --> 00:08:25.149
and they can move that up into the ceiling.

175
00:08:25.149 --> 00:08:26.670
And if I had an object for that,

176
00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:29.230
I could have it match the shape and size.

177
00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:31.589
So that's actually kind of nice to have that.

178
00:08:31.589 --> 00:08:33.549
There are a few more features in our lights

179
00:08:33.549 --> 00:08:35.659
as we look at them.

180
00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:39.120
As you can see in our spotlight,

181
00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.080
we have outer cone angle and inner cone angle control

182
00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:44.600
like you would any typical spotlight.

183
00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:47.679
There's also temperature if you wanted to use temperature.

184
00:08:48.299 --> 00:08:50.379
And does it affect world and cast shadows?

185
00:08:50.379 --> 00:08:53.220
The same thing with our point light here,

186
00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:54.919
as well as our rec.

187
00:08:54.919 --> 00:08:57.820
I can go in here and control my shadows

188
00:08:57.820 --> 00:09:00.580
and such things like does it affect the world

189
00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:02.419
and we want to use temperature.

190
00:09:02.419 --> 00:09:06.570
But you'll notice again, in that rec light that we had,

191
00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:08.450
we had that shape control

192
00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:13.210
when it came to our source width, source height,

193
00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:17.129
but we also have a thing called barn door angle

194
00:09:17.129 --> 00:09:20.190
where I can change that and almost have a focal

195
00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:22.679
or a focus area.

196
00:09:22.679 --> 00:09:25.940
Pretty nice.

197
00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:28.519
So these are all really great lights to use

198
00:09:28.519 --> 00:09:30.740
and everyone will have a different scenario

199
00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:32.799
in which you will need them.

200
00:09:32.799 --> 00:09:34.740
And again, don't mean to sound bias.

201
00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:37.139
One of my favorites is this rec light

202
00:09:37.139 --> 00:09:38.419
because it just works great

203
00:09:38.419 --> 00:09:39.899
to be able to fill light quickly

204
00:09:39.899 --> 00:09:43.519
and get it into the right position.

205
00:09:43.519 --> 00:09:46.620
Another light, which if you were paying attention

206
00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:48.100
and watching there,

207
00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:53.100
I created a couple things that we have at our disposal.

208
00:09:54.309 --> 00:09:57.159
One of them being the skylight.

209
00:09:57.159 --> 00:09:59.320
And the skylight is very helpful.

210
00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:00.399
If I turn that off,

211
00:10:00.399 --> 00:10:03.600
you'll notice we'll be losing a lot of bounce light.

212
00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:08.580
A lot of architectural companies will use this

213
00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:10.759
as a quick way to get some bounce light

214
00:10:10.759 --> 00:10:12.200
in your environment.

215
00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:13.759
And in this case using Lumen,

216
00:10:13.759 --> 00:10:15.080
you always want to make sure,

217
00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:17.840
especially if you're grabbing stuff from the marketplace,

218
00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:20.980
that the real-time capture is on.

219
00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:23.960
You can also choose whether you want a cube map

220
00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:26.200
and you can turn real-time capture off.

221
00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:27.779
And if you wanted to,

222
00:10:27.779 --> 00:10:32.779
you can do a specified cube map that you can load

223
00:10:33.059 --> 00:10:36.559
and such things as HDR Haven has some free ones

224
00:10:36.559 --> 00:10:38.659
that you can grab and load them quickly.

225
00:10:38.659 --> 00:10:40.139
That's really up to you.

226
00:10:40.139 --> 00:10:42.899
But the real-time one allows me to grab everything

227
00:10:42.899 --> 00:10:45.620
within our scene, how our clouds are set,

228
00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:48.019
how other different factors are in play,

229
00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:50.720
and it's going to all be calculated automatically.

230
00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:53.639
We can control resolution as well as threshold,

231
00:10:53.639 --> 00:10:55.600
light color, and other things

232
00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:56.919
to be able to actually get the scene

233
00:10:56.919 --> 00:10:59.679
the way that we want.

234
00:10:59.679 --> 00:11:01.399
You'll notice on each one of these lights,

235
00:11:01.399 --> 00:11:04.789
there's going to be a mobility setting.

236
00:11:04.789 --> 00:11:06.350
When you bring in a new light,

237
00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:08.750
you want to make sure that it is movable.

238
00:11:08.750 --> 00:11:10.549
Now I brought in this Rec light,

239
00:11:10.549 --> 00:11:13.549
but we want them to be calculated correctly with Lumen.

240
00:11:13.549 --> 00:11:16.340
And that requires that it's movable.

241
00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:20.659
Static is more for baking, and so is stationary.

242
00:11:20.659 --> 00:11:24.600
And Unreal will give you a preview on how those things work.

243
00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:28.929
With Lumen active, our distant mesh fields

244
00:11:28.929 --> 00:11:32.850
are already active, as well as our virtual shadow maps.

245
00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:35.840
And that's Unreal out of the box.

246
00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:37.419
But you do want to check things

247
00:11:37.419 --> 00:11:40.080
and make sure that things are going in the right direction.

248
00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:43.120
And we'll talk about that in our project settings next,

249
00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:45.600
when we look at rendering and some of the things

250
00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:50.220
you need to keep in mind when setting up Lumen.

251
00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.860
We also can go here to our point light

252
00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:55.139
and also switch this to movable.

253
00:11:55.139 --> 00:11:58.350
And that's going to be helpful.

254
00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:01.870
We also have HDR backdrop, if you wanted to use it,

255
00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:05.149
and a sun in sky setup where you're using a compass,

256
00:12:05.149 --> 00:12:06.950
and you can choose the time of day

257
00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:08.730
if you want to punch that in

258
00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:13.509
with latitude and longitude variables.

259
00:12:13.509 --> 00:12:15.629
So again, when it comes to mobility,

260
00:12:15.629 --> 00:12:18.909
static is going to be your baked indirect

261
00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:21.490
and a direct lighting.

262
00:12:21.490 --> 00:12:23.610
It is the cheapest to use.

263
00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:29.039
And that's using our older bake system using light mass.

264
00:12:29.039 --> 00:12:32.440
We also have stationary that also is to be used

265
00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:35.320
with a baking scenario.

266
00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:36.940
It's your baked indirect lighting

267
00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:38.639
and allows characters to move around

268
00:12:38.639 --> 00:12:41.919
and have shadows react to movement.

269
00:12:41.919 --> 00:12:45.159
And then that stationary will be able to be used for it

270
00:12:45.159 --> 00:12:47.399
and we'll have GI with it,

271
00:12:47.399 --> 00:12:49.679
as well as static will also have GI,

272
00:12:49.679 --> 00:12:53.899
but it will not react to any moving objects.

273
00:12:53.899 --> 00:12:56.139
You'll notice there is a limit to use max

274
00:12:56.139 --> 00:12:57.899
for overlapping lights.

275
00:12:57.899 --> 00:12:59.879
And that's really important to consider

276
00:12:59.879 --> 00:13:02.860
when it comes to working with baked lighting.

277
00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:04.019
Unreal will let you know,

278
00:13:04.019 --> 00:13:06.100
and then you'll have to manage accordingly.

279
00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:09.419
And you can see that through the viewer,

280
00:13:09.419 --> 00:13:11.480
or I should say view modes.

281
00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:14.679
The highest quality medium performance hit

282
00:13:14.679 --> 00:13:17.159
is what you'll get with this particular light.

283
00:13:17.159 --> 00:13:20.279
Now movable is the most expensive

284
00:13:20.279 --> 00:13:24.059
because that is a fully dynamic light with shadows.

285
00:13:24.059 --> 00:13:26.620
It has the highest performance cost

286
00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:28.980
and no indirect lighting.

287
00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:30.779
In our case, we're using Lumen

288
00:13:30.779 --> 00:13:32.539
and we're getting global illumination,

289
00:13:32.539 --> 00:13:33.700
which is going to be great.

290
00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:36.100
That means we're getting full bounce lighting

